ab Program Developer Early Childhood infant educator Childcare Education Supervisor Early Childhood Education Instructor Maine State Parent Ambassador/Mom of 4 Belfast Area Child Care Services Inc Child Care Program Director Oxford Sacc Childcare provider Oxford School Age and Child Care Childcare provider Infant teacher/ Oxford SACC Oxfords SACC; Toddler teacher Oxford School Age Child Care Oxford SACC School age child care Teacher at Oxford SACC Oxford SACC Program School Age Child Care Oxford SACC & Preschool School age child care provider Child care worker Oxford SACC Preschool Teacher Sacc\n\nErin Lauzier Karley OLeary Cassidy Lowell Courtnay Copeland Amanda McLaughlin Ashley Perkins Louisa Dunlap Diana Parsons Vickie Grover Meredith giresi David Motel Natalie Day Lisa Rancoutt Joshua Langtry Barbara Danforth Leifann Higgins Sessa Salas Zachary Grover Tanya Pangakis Jennifer Trudell Paula Ballesteros Tiffany Herrick\n\nGRAY Oxford Oxford Augusta Bar Harbor Oxford Belfast South Paris Buckfield South Paris Waterville Belfast Hermon Saco South Paris Oxford Camden Buckfield Waldo Phippsburg Bangor Oxford\n\nCHILDCARE DIRECTOR Oxford SACC Oxford SACC Office Admin ECCP Cook retired child care director Teachet at SACC Program Oxford SACC Childcare Worker Oxford SACC Maine State Parent Ambassador Early childhood educator Maine Parent Anbassador Director, Fairy Forest Preschool Teacher Infant teacher Oxford Sacc Preschool Director Oxford SACC Belfast Area Child Care Services Business Owner Faith Linking in Action for chilcare Oxford Sacc", "195492": "Family chum _- cm-\nAssoclation of Maine\nTestimony in Support of LD 1859: An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early\nChildhood Education by Establishing Regional Resource Hubs\nPresented to the Joint Standing Committee on Health and Human Services\nSenator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and honorable members of the Committee,\nMy name is Heather Marden, and I am submitting this testimony on behalf of the Maine\nAssociation for the Education of Young Children (MaineAEYC) and the Family Child Care Association of Maine (FCCAM). Together, our organizations represent hundreds of early childhood educators, providers, and advocates across the state who are committed to ensuring\nthat all young children in Maine have access to high-quality early care and education.\nWe strongly support LD 1859, which would establish Regional Early Childhood Learning and\nDevelopment Resource Hubs across Maine.\nMaine's families and early childhood educators have long been navigating a fragmented system that makes it challenging to access and provide consistent, high-quality early care and education.\nLD 1859 addresses these systemic issues by creating localized infrastructure that is responsive to\ncommunity needs while comiected to statewide goals.\nThe regional resource hubs proposed in this legislation will serve as critical anchors for early\nchildhood coordination, collaboration, and innovation. By embedding these hubs within existing\nregional nonprofit organizations, LD 1859 smartly avoids duplication and leverages the deep\nknowledge and relationships that these organizations already hold within their communities.\nThe duties assigned to the hubs----including regional needs assessments, parent outreach and assistance, support for educators and employers, and coordination of professional development-- are comprehensive and urgently needed. The inclusion of a whole family approach ensures that Maine is not only addressing early childhood education in isolation but is acknowledging the interconnected needs of families striving for economic stability and well-being.\nAs organizations that support both family child care providers and center-based educators, we know firsthand the barriers providers face---navigating licensing requirements, accessing\nprofessional development, managing the business side of their work, and, most importantly, finding time to focus on the children and families they serve. These hubs will provide essential,\nlocalized supports that are long overdue.\n\nImportantly, this proposal aligns with federal guidance under the Child Care and Development\nFund (CCDF). The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services strongly recommends that\nstates establish regional or statewide resource and referral centers as part of their CCDF state\nplans. These centers are recognized as essential infrastructure to help families find care, support\nproviders in improving quality, and coordinate early childhood services efficiently. LD 1859 is a\nproactive step that positions Maine to meet this federal recommendation and potentially draw\ndown future federal resources to support implementation. Currently, Maine is one of only a few states that do not offer these more local hubs of resources. Maine's resource hubs were cut\nduring the LePage Administration.\n\nChapter 1052-A: cmm mas ssnvxces\n�3738. Resource development centers (REPEALED)\nSECTION HISTORY PL 1993, c. 155, �2 (NEW). PL 1993, C. 490, �l (AVID). MRSA T. 22 �373B (RP).\n\nChild Cm-0 Rvsourcv Development Centers\nAroostook County: Child Care Express l-Still-:l1l2-7881 or (207) Ymuxtilis\nCu|nberland County (except Brunswick/HarpawelI): Child Care Connections l~888~9l7~l Hit) or (207) 871-7449\nFranklin, Oxford, and Amlroscoggin Counties: Finders Seekers l>8(l(|-543~7(l0S 0|` (Z07) 777-H387\nHancock Count y :Chil(I Care ()m>ortnnities I-Still-83441378\nor (207) ($67-9580/667-2467\nKonnebec and Southern Somerset Counties: Child (lfare\n()pti0n.<. I-800-525-2229 or (207) 626-34H)\nKnox County: (Ioastal Child Care (207) 594-5342 and for\nrek-|'t'a\\l:4 (rail l-3774584-()4G(i\nLincoln and Sngarlohoc Counties and Bronswick/Harpswell: Fznnily Focus l-8(ll)-(575-2413 or\n(207) 725-2413 ancltor referrals call l-S77-G84-(l4(~i(i\nPiscatnquis, Penobscol, and Northern Somerset Counties:\nl'<.=nqnis Child (Zaire (207) 94!-2840\nWaldo County: Beltast Area Child Care (207) I342-55337 and\nfor re-lerrztls c