childhood educators.", "195494": "'\nTestimony of Senator Henry Ingwersen\nBefore the Joint Standing Committee on Health & Human Services\n'\nLD 1859\nAn Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by\nEstablishing Regional Resource Hubs\nMay 15, 2025\n\nGood morning, Representative Meyer, and fellow members of the Joint Standing\nCommittee on Health and Human Services, I am Senator Henry Ingwersen. I\nrepresent Senate District 32, the city of Biddeford and the towns of Arundel,\nDayton, Hollis, and Lyman. I am here today to present testimony in support of LD 1859, An Act to Improve Access to Chjld Care and Early Childhood\nEducation by Establishing Regional Resource Hub.\n\nWe all understand that parents need child care to go to work. We understand that Maine businesses need a stable workforce. We understand that early childhood\neducators need to make a living wage, or we won't have educators employed at .\n\nchild care centers.\n\n_\n\nWe know that 69 percent of families in Maine with a child under age six have all\nadults in the household working, which equates to approximately 50,000 children in need of child care.` Unfortunately, child care can be difficult to find\nand afford for too many families, According to the U.S. Department of Labor, the\naverage annual cost of child care for an infant in Maine costs over $12,500. Parents with two children under the age of five must expend an extraordinary percentage of their budget to afford child care.\n\nWhile affording child care is difficult, finding Q; available child care is a\nchallenge for most families. We hear of long wait lists and families working\npart-time or not at all because they cannot find child care.\n\nWhile there is help online through the state's Child Care Choices website, Maine must provide additional support for young families. Our early childhood system\n\n' The Annie E. Casey Foundation, KIDS COUNT Data Center, Children under age 6 with all available parents in work force (5-yr averages) in\n\nMaine, retrieved from:\n\nhttg_sI//datacenter.aec�0rg/data/tabl�_�L']3 l7-children-under-a ge-6-with-all-zwailable-parents-in-work-force-detailed'?loc=j2 I &loct=2i�gletai1ed/2/ml\n\nXlfillfie/Z5`liJ 995-@3-5HflZ9-\n\n.573_859\n\nis a patchwork of programs that cross state departments and funding streams. Parents need help navigating what child care is available that meets their needs.\nFamilies may qualify for supports to pay for the care, yet too many parents are\nunaware of potential financial assistance that could help them lower the cost of\nchild care.\nThis bill aims to improve how Maine supports the child care needs of families,\neducators, businesses, and our communities. Maine is one of just three states in the country that does not utilize what is nationally called a \"child care resource and referral\" service. Maine first created a regional system of supports called\nResource Development Centers beginning in 1986, with six regional centers across the state. Over the years, these Resource Development Centers expanded\nto eleven. In 2012, the Department of Health and Human Services eliminated the\ncontracts that had in large part supported the regional centers.\nThis bill aims to create a resource that caters to the needs of parents, educators,\nand the business community in 2025, while also leveraging existing resources.\nThis bill seeks to develop foundational needs related to child care:\n\nAssisting families to find and make informed child care choices:\n0 Connecting families to resources to pay for child care and with other needed services;\n0 Increasing the supply and quality of care; 0 Establishing and coordinating community and business partnerships; and 0 Collecting and distributing child care data to improve supply\n\nA\n\nSupporting Families\n\n-\n\nTrained staff who know their communities can help parents meet the unique\nneeds of individual children and families. Information relating to child care\nquality and the state's Rising Stars for ME program can help parents make the\ninformed choices about which early childhood program will meet their needs.\nStaff can also connect parents to other significant public resources that may\nsupport the entire family.\n\nPaying for Child Care\nRegional hub staff can help parents determine eligibility for programs such as Head Start, the Child Care Affordability Program, public preschool, or tax credits. Parents would receive support navigating financial requirements and program\n'\napplications.\nIncreasing Supply of Child Care\nCommunities, and we as legislators, require better information to support the\nneeds of families and educators. We have all heard about a significant lack of\nchild care in our state. Yet we don't have good data on supply and demand as it relates to child care. Early childhood programs are made up of a mix of public\nand private entities, ranging from large centers to small home--based programs, to informal family, friend, and neighbor care. They are structured as sole proprietorships, small businesses, or nonprofit organizations. By supporting regional assessments, local communities, supported by the state, can start to address child care needs based on data and community input. Regional hubs can work to increase the workforce and support the coaching and business needs of\nnew child care professionals.\nSome communities in the state are moving ahead with local solutions. In\nAroostook County, the United Way and the Aroostook County Action Program\nare working with the business community to support the region's child care\nneeds. In Portland, the United Way and the Greater Portland Chamber of\nCommerce are working together to create more available child care. As a state, we should work with communities to develop a framework so we can gather data and\nhelp create a system that can ultimately improve access to child care, regardless of where you live in Maine.\nA strong foundation in the early years is what drives positive outcomes later in life. We must work together to ensure that all children and families have access to\nthe resources they need for a strong start. When children can get a strong early\nstart, and their parents can be fully engaged in the workforce, our communities and economy will also reap the rewards of this investment.\nThank you for your time and consideration. I'm happy to answer any questions\nyou may have about this legislation.\n\nCCR&RS ARE THE\nOF AMERICA'S CHILD CARE SYSTEM\n\nF AMERICA\n\nLocated in 47 states, Child Care Resource and Referral agencies (CCR&Rs)\nserve as resource hubs for families, child care professionals and communities.\nBelow includes services that are most commonly offered:\n\nl\nE@l\"�3\nFAQ\nLulu\nfl\n\nCCR&Rs help families find quality child care\nCCR&Rs help families navigate complex child care systems to ensure that they find high--quality, culturally responsive child care that meets their unique needs. CCR&Rs serve as a single point of access for:\n~ Child care referrals and consumer education. - Child care financial assistance programs. ~ Child care, Head Start, preschool programs, and out of school time programs. ~ Services for children with special needs.\nCCR&Rs support early childhood educators and child care programs\nCCR&Rs build the supply of quality child care within their state and local communities.\nServices offered include: - Providing mentoring, coaching, and technical assistance at child care programs both in-person andvirtually. - Developing quality improvement plans and supporting the programs through the implementation of the plans. ~ Providing business training to child care programs to ensure that their businesses are sustainable. ~ Disseminating funding to child care programs. - Equipping early childhood educators with professional supports, education, resources, and grants to ensure that they are prepared to offer specialized care and education to the children in their care.\nCCR&Rs support the vvhole family\nCCR&Rs prioritize placing children in safe, high-quality child care settings and provide additional resources and\n~\nsupports to strengthen the whole family. Services include: - Connecting families to services such as health insurance, food assistance programs, and child care health and mental health consultants. - Linking providers to program supports such as assistance with child development screenings and serving as Child and Adult Care Food Program (CACFP) sponsors.\n\n*lnsights gathered from CCAoA's 2022 CC R&R Landscape Survey.\n\nchildcareawareorg\n\n(.`CR&Rs Are the Eyes and Ears ofAmerica's Child Care System\n\n%\nF?-ii\nta\n\nCCR&Rs support child care and development block grant (CCDBG)\nimplementation\nCCR&Rs are supported by CCDBG funding and play an essential role in leading and coordinating each state's child care\nsystem. ln this role, CCR&Rs improve the quality and consistency of services for families, early childhood educators and\ncommunities. As a result, most CC R&Rs are vital resources for successful CCDBG implementation. Services include:\n~ Delivering training on critical health and safety topics required for child care programs to accept children whose\ncare is being funding by CCDBG dollars.\n~ Administering child care financial assistance (subsidy) programs to allow low-income families the opportunity to select the best child care program for their family's needs without concern of financial barriers.\nCCR&Rs partner with state lead agencies\nCCR&Rs have deep experience collaborating with state lead agencies to meet the emergent needs of children, families,\nand providers. This includes: ~ Responding to emergency situations by quickly dispersing personal protective equipment (PPE), sanitization and mitigation supplies, and other resources. - Sharing meaningful feedback to support the equitable distribution of funding. ~ Conducting targeted outreach and raising awareness of grant opportunities. ~ Standing up grant application portals. ~ Providing technical assistance and resources to child care providers to complete grant applications. ~ Executing delivery of funds to child care programs. - Reporting up-to-date child care data.\n\n.~'i\ntree]\nl---\nd\"`q\n`\n?@@�\nISSUES\n\nCCR&Rs collect, analyze and disseminate data\nCCR&Rs maintain relationships with child care programs and families. These personal relationships allow them to\nmaintain accurate, up-to-date and comprehensive child care databases. Their extensive data is used to: ~ Identify and seeking supports for underserved populations. ~ Pinpointing child care supply and demand needs and mobilizing communities to find solutions to the identified needs. - Informing important decision-making around topics such as child care funding and licensing regulations at the state and local levels. ~ Providing important data that fuels CCAoA's national research and advocacy efforts.\nCCR&Rs engage business and community stakeholders\nCCR&Rs strengthen communities by building connections within their communities.\n- Serving as a connection point between early learning programs, including Head Start, Early Head Start, Pre-K, out of school time care and child care communities.\n~ Facilitating public/ private sector partnerships to increase investments in child care. ~ Assisting business leaders in implementing policies and supports for their employee's child care needs.\nCC R&Rs across the country have reported that the top 5 issues they prioritize in\ntheir work are:\n\n- Child Care Business Practices - Family Child Care Supports - Health and Mental Health - Child Care Advocacy - Racial Equity\n\nTo find the CC R&R that services your community,\nvisit childcareawareorg or call 800.424.2246.\n\n*lnsights gathered from CCAoA's 2022 CCR&R Landscape Survey.\n\nchildcareawareorg", "195488": "R D S tggg,\n\nLU\n\nac W\n\nZ C)\n\nTO � QUALITY\nPRGEESSIONAL DEVELOPMENT NET\\X/ORK\n\nMEPO B02 9300\n\nPort l an d ,\n\n0410 -9300\n\nass-900-0055\n\nTestimony of Jenn Wiles, Early Childhood Quality Improvement Systems Coordinator, Maine Roads to Quality Professional Development Network\nComment on LD 1859, An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood\nEducation by Establishing Regional Service Hubs\nJoint Standing Committee on Health and Human Services\nMay15, 2025\nSenator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and Honorable Members of the Health and Human Services Committee, thank you for your time today. My name is Jenn Wiles, and I\nam the Early Childhood Quality Improvement Systems Coordinator at Maine Roads to Quality Professional Development Network. MRTQ PDN works to promote and support\nprofessionalism in the field of early childhood education and out-of-school time and to improve quality through the provision of a statewide system of professional development.\nWe are a partnership of the Catherine Cutler Institute at the University of Southern Maine,\nthe University of Maine Center for Community Inclusion and Disability Studies, and the\nMaine Afterschool Network that is funded primarily by the Maine Department of Health and\nHuman Services, Office of Child and Family Services, Child Care Development Block\nGrant.\nLD 1859 proposes to improve access to child care and early education through the provision of regional hubs that offer support services to families, employers, communities,\nand educators. To inform your consideration of this bill, I want to share with you that MRTQ PDN currently delivers services aimed at increasing both access to and quality of early\nchildhood settings in all regions of Maine. Most relevant to the activities outlined in LD1859\nis that MRTQ PDN provides:\n~ The database and website for Child Care Choices, Maine's online search tool for families searching for early childhood and out-of-school time care.\n0 Support to new and expanding child care businesses through training (\"Getting\nStarted in Family Child Care\" and Child Care Leadership Institute trainings) and\ntechnical assistance support.\n0 Guidance for educators navigating state systems, such as the Child Care Affordability Program, Child Care Licensing, the Child Care EducatorWage Supplement Program, and Rising Stars for ME.\n0 Training and technical assistance for educators on cultural competence and\ncreating equitable early childhood settings. MRTQ PDN has eight regionally based\nQuality Specialists available to offer phone, email, and on-site technical assistance. v Collaboration and support for regional child care-related conferences and\nprofessional development opportunities, including recent events in York and\nAroostook Counties.\n\n0 D S R cg 5 4,\n\nLL!\n\nh-\n\nZ\n\n�To QUALITY\n\nPROEESSIONAL DEVELOPMENT NETWORK\n\nPO Box 9300 Portland, ME 04104-9300\nass-900-0055\n\n0 Quarterly meetings with higher education representatives focused on the needs of\n\nearly childhood students in higher education, as well as creating alternative\n\npathways to professional achievement beyond a two- or four-year degree.\n\n0 Grants and scholarships for individuals and programs, including Quality improvement Awards specifically focused on increasing programs' Star rating with\nRising Stars for ME.\n\no The MRTQ Registry, which contains comprehensive, longitudinal data around the\nearly childhood and out-of-school workforce in Maine.\n\nAs the Legislature continues promoting policy and investments to strengthen early\nchildhood education in Maine, please see MRTQ PDN as a resource to policymakers and\nprograms. We appreciate the opportunity to share our testimony with you today and look\nforward to continuing to share our expertise in delivering professional development and quality improvement supports to early childhood educators and children and families throughout Maine.", "10055849": "May 15, 2025\nTestimony of Katie Soucy on behalf of Starting Strong In support of LD 1859: An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education\nby Establishing Regional Resource Hubs\nSenator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and members of the Health and Human Services Committee, my name is Katie Soucy and I am the director of Starting Strong, a coalition in Portland working to improve outcomes for young children and their families. I'm here today to express our strong support for LD 1859, which would establish regional early childhood resource hubs--an approach we know works, because we've seen it in action.\nStarting Strong is proud to be one of Maine's First4ME pilot sites, and our experience leading this work in Cumberland County is directly relevant to what this bill proposes. Through First4ME, we've helped implement a regional model of care coordination, family engagement, and provider support--building a local infrastructure that bridges gaps between families, providers, and systems. It's made a real difference.\nLD 1859 takes the lessons of First4ME and scales them statewide, by directing DHHS to establish regional hubs affiliated with existing nonprofit partners. These hubs would:\n Conduct regional needs assessments,\n Help families find and apply for child care,\n Connect families to local and state resources,\n Support early educators with training and shared services, and\n Collaborate with employers and school districts to respond to local demand.\nThis kind of coordination is not a luxury--it's a necessity. In Portland, even with a relatively high number of providers, families still face long waitlists, complex application processes, and significant cost burdens. In more rural parts of Cumberland County and across Maine, the challenge is even greater. Many areas are considered child care deserts, where the supply of care falls far short of the need.\nWe also know that 67% of Maine children under age six have all available parents in the workforce--yet too many of those parents are sidelined or forced into part-time work simply because they can't find care. This isn't just a family issue--it's a workforce and economic development issue.\n\nThrough our First4ME work, we've seen how impactful it is to have a trusted, regional hub where families can get support, and where providers can connect to professional development, financial assistance, and operational guidance. What LD 1859 does is formalize that model and ensure it's available in every region of the state.\nBy building on the First4ME framework and grounding this effort in existing community-based organizations, LD 1859 avoids duplication, strengthens local partnerships, and addresses one of the root causes of Maine's child care crisis: lack of coordinated infrastructure.\nOn behalf of Starting Strong and our partners across Cumberland County, I urge your support for LD 1859. It's a smart, scalable approach to improving access, building supply, and supporting both families and educators statewide.\nThank you for your leadership and your time today.\nRespectfully submitted, Katie Soucy Director, Starting Strong\nksoucy@portlandconnected.org", "10055624": "Testimony in Support of LD 1859 \"An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing Regional Resource Hubs\" Before the Joint Standing\nCommittee on Health and Human Services Senator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and Honorable Members of the Health and\nHuman Services Committee\nDear Senator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and Honorable Members of the Health and Human Services Committee:\nMy name is Kiersten S. Purington and I am the President & CEO of the Aroostook Partnership in Caribou, ME. The Aroostook Partnership is a unique private/public consortium of over 65 businesses, the Northern Maine Development Commission, County higher education institutions, and several regional non-profit organizations. Our mission is to ensure the economic strength, growth, and resiliency of Aroostook County.\nWe in the Partnership believe that LD-1859 \"An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing Regional Resource Hubs\" is critical to helping Maine's overall economy. Here in Aroostook County, we are experiencing a true crisis surrounding childcare which is reflected in other communities across the state of Maine. Although childcare issues are traditionally viewed through a social lens, the lack of available and affordable childcare significantly and negatively impacts the health of our economy. A lack of access to affordable, high-quality childcare is among one of the most pressing barriers to workforce participation and retention in Aroostook County resulting in an inability to contribute meaningfully to the regional and state economy.\nOver the last year, the Partnership has supported the development of the Aroostook Child Care Resource Center (ACCRC), a vital initiative led by the United Way of Aroostook. This emerged from a grassroots effort to address the lack of availability and affordability of childcare in The County. Research conducted by a committee consisting of a cross-section of employers, childcare providers, and educational institutions revealed that our childcare system is extremely fragile with not enough capacity to serve a growing and necessary demand. Many childcare providers do not have the necessary support in place as reflected by the fact that childcare is a \"cottage industry\" with no operational or functional framework.\nThe ACCRC is a salient example of an early childhood learning and development \"resource hub\" and aligns strongly with the proposal delineated in LD-1859. It is designed to build a strong provider network, assist with training, development and licensing, expands the childcare workforce via partnerships with local educators, and serves as an important information source for parents and families to make informed childcare decisions. Aroostook Partnership is committed to supporting this effort and we see the ACCRC as an excellent model of how addressing this crisis can strengthen the economic conditions of northern Maine by promoting family stability, workforce availability, and community well-being.\nwww.aroostookpartnership.org\n11 West Presque Isle Rd., PO Box 779, Caribou, ME 04736 Office: (207) 554-0680, Mobile: (207) 768-1342, TTY: (207) 762-1088\n\nPassing this bill will be greatly beneficial for the economic health of Aroostook County and the State of Maine. Thank you for considering our perspectives and for supporting this important initiative. Please do not hesitate to contact my office should you require additional information or support. Sincerely,\nKiersten S. Purington President & CEO Aroostook Partnership Dated 13 May 2025\n\nEqual Opportunity Lender/Agency\n\nwww.nmdc.org\n\n11 West Presque Isle Rd., PO Box 779, Caribou, ME 04736\n\nVoice: (207) 498-8736, Toll Free in ME: 1-800-427-8736, Fax: (207) 493-3108, TTY: (207) 498-6377", "195491": "~�~iw/,4 /-/\n` '\"`\n1:1!\n\n// /~ %\n\n_.\n\n'2\n\n.-\n\n.\n1\n\nI 12/ g~ .>:/ax;\n\nEouo/we MAINE\n\nTESTIIVIONY BY KIM RUSSELL, STATE DIRECTOR, READYNATION\nTO THE JOINT STANDING COMMITTEE ON HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES\nTHURSDAY, May 15, 2025, 10 A.M. IN SUPPORT OF\nLD 1859 AN ACT TO INIPROVE ACCESS TO CHILD CARE AND EARLY CHILDHOOD EDUCATION BY ESTABLISHING REGIONAL RESOURCE HUBS\n\nChairs lngwersen, and Meyer, and distinguished members of the Health and Human Services Committee, I am Kim Russell, State Director of ReadyNation. ReadyNation is a business leader organization dedicated to\nbuilding a skilled workforce and strong economy by promoting evidence-based policy solutions that prepare children to succeed in education, work, and life. Since August 2024 we have operated under Educate Maine.\n\nI am testifying in support of LD 1859 AN ACT TO IMPROVE ACCESS TO CHILD CARE AND EARLY CHILDHOOD EDUCATION BY ESTABLISHING REGIONAL RESOURCE HUBS.\n\nAs you will hear many times today, child care is a vital component of Maine's economy and workforce. Working parents cannot work without access to high-quality affordable child care. Yet the systems in place do\n\nnot make finding child care easy. For too many parents -- the current state support for helping find child care is\n\na website. While\n\n_Q_i'g_ii<,i Care Cl\"toices or Access Maine are resources that share the availability of child\n\ncare slots within various communities for licensed childcare facilities, including family child care providers,\n\ncenters, and Head Start programs -- they do not replace a live person to help parents better understand the\n\nI\n\nsystem, answer questions, and do proper outreach and make application/s to local providers. The websites are\n\nalso not helpful if they are not regularly updated.\n\nAccording to the most recent Kids Count Data Book, 69 percent of Maine children under the age of 6 have all\navailable parents in the workforce. Their ability to access reliable child care is critical.\n\nThis bill proposes to help parents and communities with this process by establishing four regional early\nchildhood learning and development resource hubs by November 1, 2025. These hubs will be affiliated with\nexisting regional nonprofit organizations to promote early childhood learning, support family needs, and increase access to high-quality early childhood education. These hubs will enhance coordination within each region by functioning as a centralized point of information and resources, will support working families by providing a reliable and up-to-date resource for child care availability and applying to child care programs, and\nshould serve to address the needs we have in so many rural areas that are child care deserts. Key functions also\nincludes conducting regional needs assessments, connecting families to local and state resources, supporting child care educators, and collaborating with employers and programs to meet local child care needs.\nLD 1859 represents a needed strategic approach to strengthening Maine's early childhood infrastructure,\nensuring that families have the support they need to access quality child care and education services. I urge\nCommittee members to support this proposal.\n\n481 Congrt as St., H303 / Portland, ME 04101/207-751-0506\n\nQ , egw ~,~\n\n\"V\n\n**\"\"'�\nz\n\"'z~./-4\"\n\nr\" \"9\n\nM-�w7;*\n\n�/'\n\n,/,atM%\n\n..,, �>\n\nW~ �~7_'\":~`.:1>:f:$�f\"%'@W`_'5�:>v>~�-1�->�~:;;~W�`:ni:\n\n\"z\" --\n\n\\~ I\n\n_\n\nM 7\"\" -- ' ~~~'\n\nI\n\n~a;.i\n\n\">t:�:a:>~\"\"\n\n,, �Q;�~@,-~ V �\n\nA rflz, J. ,.W-<\n\n~ 1� :.:'i;-\n\"\"'\"Jfl7_\n,\n\nT ~\n\n\"\n--1\n\n,_;_;.a_,.\n\n~ ~=@a*�>�>~'�<<='�;�' .. ,.", "10055856": "Executive Committee\nPresident Brian Youth, MD, FAAP\nVice President Anne Coates, MD, FAAP\nTreasurer Jeffrey Stone, DO, FAAP\nSecretary Genevieve Whiting, MD, FAAP\nImmediate Past President Laura Blaisdell, MD, MPH, FAAP\nBoard of Directors\nJoseph Anderson, DO, FAAP Rebecca Brakeley, MD, FAAP Amy Buczkowski, MD, FAAP Melissa Burch, MD, FAAP Adrienne Carmack, MD, FAAP Gabriel Civiello, MD, FAAP Alyssa Goodwin, MD, FAAP Allison Grover, MD* Deborah Q. Hagler, MD, MPH, FAAP Dan Hale, MD, FAAP Jennifer Jewell, MD, MS, FAAP Stephanie Joy, MD, FAAP Emily Keller, MD, FAAP Alton Kremer, MD, PhD, FAAP Michele LaBotz, MD, FAAP Lawrence Losey, MD, FAAP Sophia Martens** Christopher Motyl, DO, FAAP Valerie O'Hara, DO, FAAP Calvin Schaffer, MD* Andrea Tracy, MD, FAAP Lara Walsh, MD, FAAP Afnan Yahya** Margaret Zamboni, DO, FAAP\n*Resident Board Representatives **Medical Student Representatives\nStaff\nDee Kerry, BS Executive Director\nEmily Belanger, RN, BSN Education & Membership Manager\nMadeleine DesFosses, BA Public Health & Advocacy Manager\nTiffany Harrington, MBA Development Director\n30 Association Drive, Box 190 Manchester, ME 04351 office: 207-622-3374\nwww.maineaap.org\n\nTestimony in SUPPORT of: � LD 1736, An Act to Increase the Supply of Child Care Services Through the Use of Contracts � LD 1955, An Act to Increase Child Care Affordability and Early Childhood Educator Stability in Maine � LD 1859, An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education\nMay 15, 2025 Health and Human Services Committee\nSenator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and Honorable Members of the Health and Human Services Committee,\nMy name is Madeleine DesFosses, and I am submitting this testimony on behalf of the Maine Chapter of the American Academy of Pediatrics. The Maine AAP is a professional organization of 300 pediatricians and pediatric subspecialists dedicated to protecting the health of Maine children and adolescents. We are in support of LD 1736, LD 1955, and LD 1859 which will take meaningful action to support and strengthen Maine's child care system for Maine children, families, and early childhood educators.\nPediatricians support patients and families, not just along their healthcare journey, but through the myriad of challenges they face in daily life. What has become very common, but is completely unacceptable in this day and age, is for families to not have the means to afford basic child care or for them not to have access to quality care. Sadly, many Maine families are struggling to survive, and cannot afford the high cost of child care, or afford to live on only one salary. It is well documented that highquality early education and child care is essential to assuring positive physical and cognitive outcomes for children, and can set them up for success throughout their life.\nThese three bills take important steps to address the child care crisis and ensure that all Maine families have access to quality, affordable care.\nLD 1736 requires the use of direct contracts between the Office of Child and Family Services and child care providers to create availability for infants and toddlers, children with disabilities, and children in underserved areas - many of whom typically struggle to find child care. Families and children of all abilities, ages, and regions deserve access to affordable child care, and by providing contracts instead of stipends, child care for those families can become more consistent and predictable.\nLD 1955 directly addresses the affordability of care for families and the instability and chronic undervaluing of the child care workforce. This legislation would create targeted investments in affordability, wage supplements for child care professionals, and other assistance and incentives for child care professionals. Child care is often the largest monthly expense for working families, costing an average of $10,000 per\n\ninfant each year. We must work to make child care more affordable for more families, and compensate educators fairly to create a more sustainable workforce that Maine families can depend on.\nLD 1859 seeks to establish regional early childhood learning and development resource hubs through the Department of Health and Human Services. These hubs would partner with existing nonprofit organizations to promote early childhood education and support families with finding child care. In locations where child care is extremely limited, these hubs will help to meet the needs of families and children, and lead to better learning outcomes.\nPlease help to ensure our children, families, child care providers, and Maine's economy thrive, and vote \"Ought to Pass\" on LD 1736, LD 1955, and LD 1859.\nThank you for your time and consideration.\nMadeleine DesFosses, Advocacy and Public Health Manager, Maine AAP", "10055784": "Testimony of Meg Helming before the Committee on Health and Human Services\nin support of LD 1859: An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing\nRegional Resource Hubs May 15, 2025\nSenator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and distinguished members of the Health and Human Services Committee, my name is Meg Helming and I'm the Chief Operating Officer at the YMCA Alliance of Northern New England, which represents the 15 YMCAs in Maine.\nWe strongly support of LD 1859, which proposes the creation of regional early childhood learning and development resource hubs. These hubs would play a vital role in strengthening the early childhood ecosystem by addressing the interconnected needs of families, providers, educators, and employers. YMCAs across Maine are deeply embedded in their local communities and are proud to the largest provider of early care and education in the state. We see firsthand the persistent challenges families face in finding affordable, high-quality care, and the systemic pressures that make it difficult for providers to sustain and grow their programs. LD 1859 addresses these realities in several critical ways:\n� Coordination and Navigation: Families often struggle to understand and access the services and supports available to them. By serving as regional navigators, these hubs would help families locate childcare, connect with public programs like CCAP and Head Start, and access communitybased services that support whole-family well-being.\n� Regional Planning and Partnership: The requirement that each hub conduct a regional needs assessment and convene partners across sectors--including schools, employers, and health systems--will ensure that local solutions are informed by local data and collaboration.\n� Support for Providers and Workforce: From shared services models to professional development and technical assistance, the hubs would directly support providers in expanding their capacity and navigating complex state systems--helping retain and grow a stable early childhood workforce.\n� Public-Private Leverage: The proposed 50% matching requirement and local partner boards demonstrate a smart model of shared responsibility, encouraging both public investment and community ownership.\nIn short, LD 1859 represents a forward-thinking, place-based strategy to improve the coordination, quality, and reach of Maine's early childhood services. YMCAs across the state are ready and willing to serve as partners in this work, and we believe these resource hubs can serve as a foundational element in advancing equity and access for Maine's children and families.\nWe respectfully urge the committee to vote \"Ought to Pass\" on LD 1859. Thank you for your commitment to Maine's youngest learners.\nSincerely,\nMeg Helming\nContact: Meg Helming, YMCA Alliance of Northern New England P: 207.232.9896 E: meg.helming@nneymcas.org", "195493": "\\`\n\n% F\n\nuumzu WAY\n\nAioostouk\n\nAroostook County\n\nChildcare Resource Center\nQ An Initiative of United Way ofAroostook\n'\n5g]\n@\n\n2 O-]_768_O767\nrnelanie@unitedWayaroostookorg\naroostool<corc.org\n\nLD 1859, An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing Regional Resource Hubs\nPresented to Maine's Joint Standing Committee on Health and Human Services\n\nSenator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer and honorable members of the Health and Human Services Committee,\nAs the Director of the Aroostook County Childcare Resource Center I ask you to support LD 1859 an Act to Improve\nAccess to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing Regional Resource Hubs.\n\nLack of accessible and affordable childcare is a growing economic problem for rural Maine. It is often cited as the top barrier to employment and acutely impacts the next generation of our workforce. Making matters worse, our current childcare providers are struggling to keep their doors open due to lack of staffing, regulatory burden, unpredictable revenue and increasing expenses. This has resulted in a brittle system that we collectively need to address.\n\nAroostook County has been actively seeking solutions to the childcare crisis and one solution that has been implemented is the Aroostook County Childcare Resource Center, which aligns impeccably with this proposed legislation.\n\nThe Aroostook Resource Center was formed by a local group of stakeholders out of the childcare crisis in Aroostook\nCounty. We are designed as an initiative under the United Way of Aroostook, which has allowed us to be a neutral party in childcare and to leverage the stability and relationships the United Way has within the community. We operate under an advisory board which consists of providers, parents, businesses, Aroostook's CAP program, higher education, and financial\ninstitutions. Our mission includes supporting families, providers, businesses and the community to foster a resilient childcare\ninfrastructure. All of our current funding is locally sourced from private contributions. We have applied for Congressionally\nDirected Spending to expand our efforts with recruiting and training the childcare workforce, which will directly impact the availability and quality of care offered to children and families.\nMost of our early work has focused on stabilizing ourcurrent providers by assisting with recruiting staff and leveraging financial and business support. Our work with families has mostly centered on finding creative solutions to childcare as there are rarely any open slots anywhere in Aroostook, especially after the loss of two large centers and other providers leaving the\nfield. We have also encouraged and supported families with applying for CCAP, despite the ongoing waitlist.\nThe Resource Center collaborates closely with First 4 ME and has been developing relationships with all available resources\nfor families and providers throughout the state. We also work closely with licensing and other programs through OCFS to\noffer assistance to current and hopeful providers. These connections are vital to support our most rural childcare providers,\nas they may not always know what is out there or how to access the support.\nChildcare is a complex system that impacts every aspect of our community and economy. Hubs like the Aroostook Resource Center will support stabilizing and expanding providers to meet the needs of families and businesses, while emphasizing the\nquality of care our children receive.\n\nBest,\nMelanie Tompkins, LCSW Director, ACCRC\n\n' M\n\n--'\n\nwere\n\n--\n\n-~", "10055921": "Maine Developmental Disabilities Council\n\nMay 15, 2025\n\nDear Senator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and Members of the Joint Standing Committee on Health and Human Services: please accept this testimony Support of LD 1859, An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing Regional resource Hubs.\n\nMy name is Nancy Cronin, and I am the Executive Director of the Maine Developmental Disabilities Council1. I stand here in support of LD 1859 as it fills a critical need for families to be able to access childcare and preschool programs. There is no doubt in our mind that this bill fills a critical gap.\n\nI encourage the committee to formalize a connection between this regional hub and the regional support and service hubs that were codified within Title 20-A, Part 4 �1 Chapter 203 Sub 1 �7209A.5. two years ago. That statute defines the transition of Child Developmental Services early education 3-5 programs for eligible children with disabilities to be conducted and overseen by local school districts. After the transition to the local school system the Child Developmental Services office is to transform into regional support and service hubs to support schools to fulfill their duties.\n\nThese two regional hubs will complement each other as long as they are coordinated. The families whose children receive services through early education programs will also need childcare. It is important that the two regional support hubs talk together to ensure coordinated support for all families. I encourage this committee to, however appropriately, make clear that these two regional service hubs should formally coordinate.\n\nThank you,\n\nSincerely,\n\nNancy Cronin Maine Developmental Disabilities Council.\n1 Councils on Developmental Disabilities were created through the Developmental Disabilities Assistance and Bill of Rights Act (DD Act) in 1970. Maine's DD Council has been advocating with and for individuals with developmental disabilities for over 50 years. The DD Council is a federally funded, independent organization with members from across the state, including persons with disabilities, family members, and representatives of public and private agencies which provide services and/or funding for services for individuals with developmental disabilities. As required in federal law, we are involved in advocacy, capacity building and systemic change activities, with the goal that individuals with developmental and other disabilities of all ages are fully included, integrated, and involved in their communities and the decisions impacting their lives.\n225 Western Avenue, Suite 4, Augusta, Maine 04330 207-287-4213 800-244-3990 www.maineddc.org", "195490": "Maine Children's Alliance\nAdvocating for the welI~being of all Maine families.\nTestimony of Rita Furlow\nBefore the Joint Standing Committee on Health & Human Services\nLD 1859 An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing\nRegional Resource Hubs May 15, 2025\nGood morning, Senator Ingwersen, Representative Meyer, and members of the Joint\nStanding Committee on Health and Human Services, I am Rita Furlow. I represent Senate District 32, the city of Biddeford and the towns of Arundel, Dayton, Hollis, and Lyman. I am\nhere today to present testimony in support of LD 1859, An Act to Improve Access to Child Care and Early Childhood Education by Establishing Regional Resource Hub. The Maine Children's Alliance is a statewide non-partisan, non-profit research and advocacy organization whose mission is to promote sound public policies to improve the lives of children, youth, and families in Maine.\nBecause the brain's architecture is built over time, and from the bottom up, ensuring a\nstrong foundation early in life is critical to everything that follows. Strong foundations are built by making sure young children have positive learning experiences and stable,\nconsistent relationships with caring adults at home and in the community. Today, with 69% of Maine children under age six having all resident parents in the workforce, it is crucial to make sure Maine's investments in early childhood reflect the science of what\nchildren need, while also accommodating Maine families as they raise the generation that will lead Maine into the future.`\nWe are supportive of this legislation because it would support the needs of parents\nsearching for child care, support new and existing child care providers, and work with the business community to meet the child care needs of their employers.\nMany of you are likely to vividly recall the first time you dropped off your first-born at a child care center. Many parents need help navigating which child care center orfamily child care home is best for their child. Sometimes the first placement doesn't work for a family. Parents across the country receive much more support than parents in Maine when they\nare facing the search for child care. Most states in the country, 47 according to the national\n1 The Annie E. Casey Foundation, KIDS COUNT Data Center, Children under age 6 with all available parents in work force (5-yr averages) in\nMaine, retrieved from: htlps://dalacenter.aecf.org/data/tables/73 I 7-chiIdren~under-age-6-with-alI-avaiIabIe-parenls~in-work-forcedetaiIed?Ioc=2 I &loct=2#detailed/Zianv/false/2545_ I 095.2048.574. I 72937.87 I .87(),573,869/anv/ I 4380. I 438 I\n\norganization, Child Care Aware, support families with organizations called \"resource and\nreferral centers.\"\nAs you have all heard many times, child care is very expensive for young families. As our\nrecent KIDS COUNT Data Book notes, the average cost of child care for an infant at a child\ncare center in Maine is over $12,000 -- similar to a first year of tuition at the University of Maine. There are programs that can ease the financial burden of child care. Unfortunately,\ntoo few families seem to have information about the availability of programs such as the Child Care Affordability Program, Head Start, or tax credits that could support them in\npaying for child care. Regional support staff can assist families in notjust finding care but in navigating eligibility and program applications.\nWe also support this legislation because it can help local communities, and the state,\ndetermine their child care needs. If we are going to meet the child care needs of working families, we need better information -- at both the community and state level.\nWe know there are significant waiting lists -- yet we don't have good information on where those wait list are in the state. We regularly hear from families about the lack of availability\nof child care for infants and toddlers. Yet we don't have good information to help support\nthe growth of this care in particular communities.\nThis legislation would support the collection of data that could be used at the local and state level to better support critical investments to support the needs of families. Collecting this type of information is not the responsibility of the Department of Health and\nHuman Services. While they license child care providers and administer the Child Care\nAffordability Program, they are not tasked with understanding and supporting the supply\nand demand of the child care market.\nThis bill would also support communities by working with employers seeking child care for their employees. Regional resource hubs can support the child care needs of Maine businesses by connecting them with local child care providers or working alongside them\nwith other community partners to help grow new child care businesses.\nWe urge you to support this legislation to support the needs of families, local educations,\nand business community as they work at the local level to support the needs of children, families, and our local and state economy.\nThank you for your time and consideration. I'm happy to answer any questions you may\nhave about this legislation.\n\nThe University of Maine\nDigitalCommons@Ul\\/iaine\n\nMaine Women's Publications - All\n\nPublications\n\n7-1-1988\nInform - A Quarterly Newsletter for Maine Women (June-Aug\n1988)\nThe Maine Commission for Women Staff\nThe Maine Commission for Women\n\nFollow this and additional works at: https://digitalcommons.library.umaine.edu/maine_women_pubs_ail\nPart of the Women's History Commons\nRepository Citation\nStaff, The Maine Commission for Women, \"lnform - A Quarterly Newsletter for Maine Women (June-Aug\n1988)\" (1988). Maine Women's Publications - All. 395.\nhitps://digiialcommons.library.umaineedu/maine_women_pubs__all/395\nThis Newsletter is brought to you for free and open access by DigitalCommons@UMaine. lt has been accepted for inclusion in Maine Women's Publications - All by an authorized administrator of DigitalCommons@UMaine. For more information, please contact um.librarytechnical.services@maine.edu.\n\n3+ f)oc.$.\n\n-I 3\"l~ 2-\nIn 3\n/;r.,~ ` '33:, ' 1%,.\n\nrt\"\n\ncul-\n\nJune, July, August 83\nl)�~!V!\"�T'Y cs rum rimms\nsmr or MMNK\nDOCUMENT b�PO5l.YC'>il`t`\n\ni\n\nPul>lisl'recl by The Maine Commission for Women\n\nFROM THE CHAIR\n\nln thi� issue of lglfilgrt the Commizsion in locualng on on� oi the most important\nlasting attesting many working women\ntnrlay ~ quality. allordable child care. tfihlltl metre is an issue that is now being\n\n2lcldrt2is=:sr:rr.l by many businc-vases out nl E?C1l3l'i0lt1iG nrwrzssity. lviost women work\n\nnut cal economic necessity.\n\nSince\n\nwortinrt rnake up 53% cl today's\n\nwnrlrlnrciti, i)LlE.~ll\"lE!5SB$ are riar:ngrlizing\n\nthe economic. necessity to an-\n\nrzrinirnorlzitia child care needs.\n\nGovernor John R. McKernan, Jr. has been a leader in recognizing the need for\n\nquality, allordabtia child care. This past\n\nlegislative session he iricluded $3.5\n\nmillion in his budget to improvr; child care\n\nin ltdairte. An overviraw at the Governor's pzickage on child care is included. The\n\nrole ol the Otlice ol Child Care is high~\n\nlighted a